{
 "cells": [
  {
   "attachments": {},
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "This notebook showcases the functionality of `galCIB`. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 1,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "%load_ext autoreload\n",
    "%autoreload 2"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 2,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "import numpy as np\n",
    "import galCIB\n",
    "\n",
    "import matplotlib.pyplot as plt"
   ]
  },
  {
   "attachments": {},
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "`galCIB` is a tool that calculates the expected galaxy x galaxy, galaxy x CIB and CIB x CIB power spectra (3D or 2D), given certain parameters. \n",
    "\n",
    "It is a highly flexible model that uses a halo-model approach to connect physical properties of interest such as the cosmic star formation history, halo dust temperature, galaxy halo properties, etc. with the observed power spectra. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "attachments": {},
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "Currently, the default mode calculates the projected 2D power spectra, $C(\\ell)$, given these parameters. Mathematically, our model appears as follows: \n",
    "\n",
    "\\begin{equation}\n",
    "    C_{\\rm XY} (\\ell) = \\int_{z_{\\rm min}}^{z_{\\rm max}} \\frac{dz}{c} \\frac{H(z)}{\\chi^2(z)} W_{\\rm X} (z) W_{\\rm Y} (z) P_{\\rm XY} \\left( k = \\frac{\\ell + 1/2}{\\chi}, z \\right)\n",
    "\\end{equation}\n",
    "\n",
    "where, the left-hand side is our predicted cross-correlation between two fields, $X$ and $Y$; in our case {$X$,$Y$} $\\in$ {galaxy, CIB}. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "`galCIB` streamlines the process of calculating these integrals with flexible-user defined functions. Below we showcase each of the steps needed to build a pipeline of your choice. Each aspect of this integral is broken into classes that takes care of its own calculations. \n",
    "\n",
    "Below we showcase how to build up the analysis pipeline with `galCIB`."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Step 0: Setup `Survey` parameters.\n",
    "\n",
    "A galaxy X CIB analysis requires one to specify both the galaxy and the CIB surveys. Here are the required ingredients. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## Galaxy Survey\n",
    "\n",
    "The user specifies the redshift distribution of the galaxy sample. As an example, we use the DESI Photometric ELG sample from [Karim et al. 2025](https://arxiv.org/abs/2408.15909). The user also has to define the magnification bias of the galaxy sample. Note that we use the $\\alpha$ parameter convention as opposed to the $s$ parameter convention in `galCIB`. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 3,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 640x480 with 1 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# Define redshift distribution\n",
    "dndz_file=np.load(\"../data/minimal/gal/dndz_extended.p\",\n",
    "        allow_pickle=True)\n",
    "zs=dndz_file['zrange']\n",
    "pz=dndz_file['dndz'].mean(axis=0)\n",
    "\n",
    "zs = np.concatenate((zs,np.arange(2.85, 10.05, 0.1)[2:]))\n",
    "pz = np.concatenate((pz,np.arange(2.85, 10.05, 0.1)[2:]*0))\n",
    "\n",
    "plt.plot(zs, pz)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Define magnification bias\n",
    "mag_alpha = 2.225"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "## CIB Survey\n",
    "\n",
    "The user specifies the frequency channels to be observed and their associated filter curves. By default, we have the *Planck* $353$, $545$, and $857$ GHz channels implemented. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 4,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "nu_obs = [353, 545, 857] # Planck effective freq. in GHz\n",
    "\n",
    "# load Planck filter response curves \n",
    "from galCIB.utils.io import load_my_filters\n",
    "\n",
    "cib_filters = load_my_filters(\"../data/minimal/filters/\",\n",
    "                nu_obs=nu_obs)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Step 1: Setup `Survey`\n",
    "\n",
    "With the essential ingredients specified, we can now setup the `Survey` class. Note that as performing an angular clustering analysis, we also need to define the range of $\\ell$ to be used in the analysis and the resolution of the CIB maps given by `NSIDE`.\n",
    "\n",
    "Given these information, we setup the object `example_survey`."
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 5,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "from galCIB import Survey \n",
    "\n",
    "LMIN = 100; LMAX=2000\n",
    "ells = np.arange(LMIN, LMAX)\n",
    "NSIDE = 1024 \n",
    "\n",
    "example_survey = Survey(z=zs, \n",
    "                 pz=pz, mag_alpha=mag_alpha,  \n",
    "                 cib_filters=cib_filters,\n",
    "                 ells=ells, nside=NSIDE,\n",
    "                 name=\"Example\")"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Setup `Cosmology` \n",
    "\n",
    "Next, the user has to define what cosmology they want to perform their analysis in. `galCIB` assumes a specific cosmology to run. In the future, we may implement flexibilty to vary cosmology along with CIB parameters. \n",
    "\n",
    "`galCIB` is integrated with the [`colossus`](https://www.benediktdiemer.com/code/colossus/) package which takes care of halo-mass function-related calculations. Right now, you can specify one of the many [standard cosmologies](https://bdiemer.bitbucket.io/colossus/cosmology_cosmology.html) implemented in `colossus`. Simply pass the named string from the `ID` column to the argument `colossus_cosmo_name`. In the future we may implement user-specific cosmological parameters. \n",
    "\n",
    "We also allow the user to specify whether all the calculations should be done in $h^{-1}$ units with the boolean flag `use_little_h`. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 6,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "ks = np.logspace(-3,1,500)\n",
    "Mh = np.logspace(7,15,100)\n",
    "\n",
    "from galCIB import Cosmology\n",
    "\n",
    "cosmo = Cosmology(example_survey.z, ks, Mh,\n",
    "                  colossus_cosmo_name='planck18',\n",
    "                  use_little_h=False)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "There are lots of important calculations that are cached in `cosmo` which the user can access if they wish to. For example, you can obtain the value of the Hubble constant by running `cosmo.H0` or take a look at your Halo-Mass Function by calling `cosmo.get_hmf_grid()`. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 7,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stdout",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "67.66\n",
      "[[1.43009041e+002 1.43151793e+002 1.43523619e+002 ... 7.81893141e+001\n",
      "  7.62976205e+001 7.44253950e+001]\n",
      " [1.20183898e+002 1.20323168e+002 1.20654969e+002 ... 6.34300320e+001\n",
      "  6.18439955e+001 6.02758188e+001]\n",
      " [1.01011732e+002 1.01145191e+002 1.01440461e+002 ... 5.13816427e+001\n",
