      ]
     },
     "execution_count": 8,
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "execute_result"
    },
    {
     "data": {
      "image/png": "",
      "text/plain": [
       "<Figure size 640x480 with 1 Axes>"
      ]
     },
     "metadata": {},
     "output_type": "display_data"
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "# Calculate window \n",
    "\n",
    "example_survey.compute_windows(cosmo)\n",
    "plt.plot(cosmo.z, example_survey.Wg, label = r'$W_g$')\n",
    "plt.plot(cosmo.z, example_survey.Wmu, label = r'$W_{\\mu}$')\n",
    "plt.xlabel(\"Redshift z\")\n",
    "plt.legend(loc='best')"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Step 4: Setup `HODModel` of the galaxy sample \n",
    "\n",
    "Now we are getting into the weeds of the modeling. `galCIB` takes a halo-occupation distribution approach to measure galaxy clustering. The user can define their own HOD functions for central and satellite galaxies and pass it to the class `HODModel`. \n",
    "\n",
    "Make sure that the output of your function is of the shape ($N_{M_h}$, $N_z$). Here $N_{M_h}$ is the number of halo mass bins and $N_z$ is the number of redshift bins. \n",
    "\n",
    "We have implemented two default HOD models, the original Zheng 2005 model as `Zheng-05` and the DESI ELG HOD model as `DESI-ELG`. If you want to use either of these, you can instantiate them by calling `get_hod_model`. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 9,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "from galCIB import get_hod_model\n",
    "elg_hod_model = get_hod_model(\"DESI-ELG\", cosmo)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Step 5: Setup `SatProfile` \n",
    "\n",
    "In the halo model approach, one has to also specify the radial profile of the satellite galaxies to properly calculate the $1$-halo term of the power spectra. \n",
    "\n",
    "By default, most analyses assume an NFW profile which `galCIB` supports. We also have a mixed profile that is a mixture of the NFW and an exponential decay profile, following the characteristics of the ELG satellites in [Rocher et al. 2024](https://arxiv.org/abs/2306.06319). In this example, we use the mixed model. The description of the exponential decay parameters can be found in the cited paper. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 10,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [
    {
     "name": "stderr",
     "output_type": "stream",
     "text": [
      "/Users/destrysaul/code/galCIB/src/galCIB/satprofile/profile.py:39: UserWarning: For the default mixed profile, theta should be [f_exp, tau_exp, lambda_NFW].\n",
      "  warnings.warn(\n"
     ]
    }
   ],
   "source": [
    "from galCIB import SatProfile\n",
    "\n",
    "theta_prof = np.array([0.3, 6.14, 1]) # fexp, tau, LambdaNFW\n",
    "elg_sat_profile = SatProfile(cosmo, theta_prof, profile_type='mixed')"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "On the other hand, CIB-emitters are often modeled as following the canonical NFW profile ([Yan et al. 2023](https://arxiv.org/pdf/2310.10848)). We can use two different profiles to describe the two different galaxy populations. Since NFW profile is fixed for a given cosmology, Halo Mass Function and concentration definition, we do not have to pass any parameters to initialize it. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 11,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "cib_sat_profile = SatProfile(cosmo, None, profile_type='nfw')"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Step 6: Setup `SFRModel`\n",
    "\n",
    "The halo-model of the CIB requires a star-formation rate (SFR) model. Right now, we only have one SFR model implemented, based on the Maniyar et al. 2021 paper. In the future, we will implement flexibility to let the user define their own SFR model. \n",
    "\n",
    "The implemented SFR model also requires us to assume some HOD model for the CIB-emitting galaxies. Here, we assume that they follow the Zheng et al. 2005 paper. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 12,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "from galCIB import SFRModel\n",
    "\n",
    "# IR-emitting galaxies  HOD model \n",
    "hod_IR = get_hod_model(\"Zheng05\", cosmo)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Default SFR model\n",
    "sfr_model = SFRModel(name=\"M21\", hod=hod_IR, fsub=0.134)"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Step 7: Setup `SnuModel`\n",
    "\n",
    "Now we setup the SED model of the CIB field. We have two default models implemented, the non-parametric Maniyar et al. 2021 model, and the parametric Yan et al. 2023 model. These are implemented as `M21` and `Y23` in the package. "
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 13,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "from galCIB import SnuModel\n",
    "\n",
    "# Parametric Snu model (Y23)\n",
    "snu_model_Y23 = SnuModel(name=\"Y23\", cosmo=cosmo, survey=example_survey)\n",
    "\n",
    "# Non parametric Snu model (M21)\n",
    "# snu_model_M21 = SnuModel(name=\"M21\", cosmo=cosmo, survey=example_survey)\n",
    "\n"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Step 8: Setup `CIBModel`"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "code",
   "execution_count": 14,
   "metadata": {},
   "outputs": [],
   "source": [
    "from galCIB import CIBModel \n",
    "\n",
    "# Initialize CIB model\n",
    "cib_Y23 = CIBModel(sfr_model=sfr_model, \n",
    "                   snu_model=snu_model_Y23,\n",
    "                   hod_IR=hod_IR\n",
    "                   )"
   ]
  },
  {
   "cell_type": "markdown",
   "metadata": {},
   "source": [
    "# Step 9: Setup `PkBuilder`"
   ]
  },
